(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antivirals-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antivirals Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two conditions does varicella-zoster virus cause?","choices":["Retinitis and colitis","Infectious mononucleosis and hepatitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — primary infection and later reactivation of the same virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What route and dosing consideration apply to oseltamivir?","choices":["Available orally only, and requiring renal dose adjustment","Available intravenously only, with hepatic adjustment","Available orally and intravenously, with no adjustment needed","Available as an inhaled powder only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renal adjustment is a consideration rather than a dose to memorise. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"Which renal adverse effect is characteristic of acyclovir, and how is it mitigated?","choices":["Its intravenous vehicle accumulates in renal failure","It crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration and renal function must be maintained","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring hydration with normal saline","It causes interstitial nephritis requiring corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Note how this differs from amphotericin B, which damages the tubule directly rather than crystallising in it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which of the listed antiviral approaches works on the host rather than on the virus?","choices":["Stimulating the host immune system","Inhibiting specific viral enzymes","Blocking viral assembly","Inhibiting viral protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is the only one of the eight aimed at the host side of the interaction. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What is oseltamivir's mechanism of action?","choices":["It inhibits influenza virus neuraminidase, preventing budding progeny from being cleaved free of the host cell","It blocks viral attachment to the host cell receptor","It is a guanine analogue that terminates the viral nucleic acid chain","It inhibits a viral protease, preventing cleavage of polypeptide precursors into mature viral proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it blocks viral release rather than viral entry or replication. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A patient has meningitis attributed to a herpes simplex virus. Which type does the lecture associate with the meninges?","choices":["Neither type; meningitis is a cytomegalovirus manifestation","Neither type; meningitis is caused by varicella-zoster only","Type 2","Type 1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges; type 1 covers mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does the acyclovir family represent?","choices":["Blocking uncoating after entry","Inhibiting viral assembly","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Inhibiting viral release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the drug is incorporated into viral DNA and terminates the chain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Which non-neurologic adverse effects are listed for acyclovir?","choices":["Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol","Nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia as the dominant effects","Photosensitivity and tooth discoloration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with hydration and renal function to be maintained because of tubular crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about starting acyclovir within the first 24 hours of chickenpox?","choices":["It shortens the acute illness but does not cure the infection","It cures the infection and prevents later reactivation as shingles","It has no effect unless combined with valacyclovir","It prevents transmission to contacts but does not alter the illness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the virus is not eradicated, which is why reactivation as shingles remains possible later. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"How does acyclovir achieve selectivity for infected cells?","choices":["It is selectively phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase, so it is only activated where the virus is replicating","It is concentrated by a membrane transporter that is expressed only on the surface of infected cells","It binds a viral capsid protein that has no human counterpart","It is activated only at the low pH found inside infected cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selectivity comes from an enzyme the virus supplies, which is the cleanest form of selective toxicity in this lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Once phosphorylated and incorporated into viral DNA, how does acyclovir stop replication?","choices":["It causes helical structure loss and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and prevents supercoiling","It is a guanine analogue lacking the sugar moiety, so the chain cannot elongate past it","It cross-links the two strands of viral DNA so that they can no longer separate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chain termination by a missing sugar, which is a structural rather than an enzymatic block. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Three prodrug and parent pairs appear in the antiviral section. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Oseltamivir with acyclovir, valacyclovir with penciclovir, and famciclovir with ganciclovir","There are no prodrugs among the antivirals in this lecture","Valacyclovir with acyclovir, famciclovir with penciclovir, and valganciclovir with ganciclovir","Valacyclovir with ganciclovir, famciclovir with acyclovir, and valganciclovir with penciclovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In each case the prodrug exists to solve poor oral absorption of the parent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which of ganciclovir and valganciclovir is the better-absorbed oral form?","choices":["Ganciclovir, the parent compound","Both are equally absorbed","Neither is absorbed orally; both are intravenous only","Valganciclovir, the prodrug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ganciclovir's oral bioavailability is very low, which is why the prodrug exists. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which haematologic adverse effects limit ganciclovir?","choices":["Megaloblastic anaemia and agranulocytosis from folate antagonism","Haemolysis triggered by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Isolated eosinophilia with no clinical consequence","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and about a third of patients must stop intravenous treatment because of side effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which enzyme does each of acyclovir and oseltamivir depend on or target?","choices":["Acyclovir targets viral neuraminidase directly; oseltamivir depends on thymidine kinase for activation","Both depend on viral thymidine kinase for activation","Both target viral neuraminidase at different sites","Acyclovir depends on viral thymidine kinase to be activated; oseltamivir targets viral neuraminidase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one uses a viral enzyme to switch itself on, the other shuts a viral enzyme off. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Why must renal function be watched during acyclovir therapy?","choices":["It causes interstitial nephritis as a hypersensitivity reaction","The drug crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained","The drug is nephrotoxic through direct tubular necrosis","Its vehicle accumulates in renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a physical rather than a toxic mechanism of renal injury. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which viruses does oseltamivir target, and in what season?","choices":["Cytomegalovirus in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B, in a season running roughly October to March","Influenza A only, year-round","Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster, circulating year-round"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and resistance has been becoming a problem over time. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which sites does herpes simplex type 1 characteristically affect?","choices":["Genitals, rectum, hands or meninges","Retina, oesophagus and colon","Skin in a dermatomal distribution following chickenpox","Mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges — the split is anatomical rather than absolute. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Compared with acyclovir, what makes ganciclovir the harder drug to tolerate?","choices":["A disulfiram-like reaction on any alcohol exposure","Marked neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, with about a third of patients stopping intravenous treatment","Crystallisation within the renal tubule, requiring aggressive hydration throughout treatment","Severe infusion reactions requiring premedication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acyclovir's bone marrow suppression is listed, but ganciclovir's is far more limiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A transplant recipient develops cytomegalovirus retinitis. Why is acyclovir not the answer?","choices":["Acyclovir is available only intravenously and cannot safely be given to a transplant recipient on immunosuppression","Acyclovir causes neutropenia in a third of patients","Acyclovir has no antiviral activity at all in immunocompromised patients","Acyclovir's indications are herpes simplex and varicella-zoster; cytomegalovirus requires ganciclovir or valganciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All are guanine analogues, but the indications are drawn along virus lines rather than by drug family. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"}]};
